# Custom DnD Builder — server environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Every variable the server reads is listed here, and a test
# (src/server/env.test.ts) fails if this file and `src/server/env.ts` ever name different sets —
# so a variable added to the code without a line here is a failing build, not a support ticket.
#
# How this file reaches the server:
#   yarn dev / yarn build — `vite.config.ts` loads it into `process.env` (Vite does not do this on
#                           its own; it only exposes VITE_-prefixed keys to the *client*)
#   production            — the operator's runner supplies them, e.g. `node --env-file=.env …`.
#                           TICKET-POL-03 documents the run command.
#
# There is deliberately **no variable naming the backend** (v3 Req 47.7): the server hosts the
# client bundle, the API is addressed by relative path, and the socket derives its URL from
# window.location. A variable that could point somewhere else is a variable someone will
# eventually point somewhere else.

# PORT — optional, defaults to 3000
# Which port the built server listens on (`yarn start`). It is **not an origin**: the client
# addresses the API by relative path and the socket from window.location, so moving this moves the
# app, the API and the socket together and nothing has to be told where the others went.
PORT=3000

# HOST — optional, defaults to every interface
# Which interface to bind, e.g. 127.0.0.1. Leave it unset on a box that is only running this.
# Behind a reverse proxy, set it to loopback — otherwise the app is also reachable unencrypted on
# PORT beside the proxy serving it on 443, which is a bypass rather than a convenience.
HOST=

# DATABASE_URL — REQUIRED
# Path to the SQLite file holding every piece of server state. Relative paths resolve from the
# working directory. WAL mode means it is really three files (`app.db`, `-wal`, `-shm`), so the
# directory must be durable and is what a backup copies — see the README.
DATABASE_URL=./data/app.db

# NODE_ENV — optional; leave it blank
# Which build this is, and it decides whether the session cookie is `Secure`. **Leave it empty and
# let the command answer**: `yarn dev` is development, and `yarn start` defaults it to production.
# Filling it in here would override that — a copied `.env` saying `development` is how a production
# deployment ends up serving cookies without `Secure`, which announces itself nowhere. Set it only
# to deliberately overrule the command, e.g. a staging box behind a plain-HTTP proxy. Vite also
# refuses `NODE_ENV=production` from this file on purpose: a production build is `yarn build`.
NODE_ENV=

# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET — REQUIRED (TICKET-AUTH-01)
# The key every Auth_Session cookie is signed with. Generate one with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Changing it invalidates every cookie, which is also the way to sign everybody out at once.
# Keep it out of version control — `.env` is gitignored and this file holds a placeholder.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32

# AUTH_SESSION_DAYS — optional, defaults to 30
# The **idle** half of a session lifetime, in days. Every use pushes it out again, so this is how
# long you may stay away and still come back signed in (v3 Req 48.2). The cookie carries an explicit
# expiry, so closing the browser does not sign you out (v3 Req 48.1).
AUTH_SESSION_DAYS=30

# AUTH_SESSION_ABSOLUTE_DAYS — optional, defaults to 90
# The **absolute** ceiling, in days (v3 Req 48.3). No amount of continuous use extends a session
# past this — it is what bounds a cookie somebody stole. The two lifetimes are enforced as one
# expiry: a renewal writes whichever of `now + idle` and `signed-in-at + absolute` comes first, so
# a value below AUTH_SESSION_DAYS simply wins.
AUTH_SESSION_ABSOLUTE_DAYS=90

# AUTH_SESSION_UPDATE_HOURS — optional, defaults to 24
# How often a session in use is renewed and its identifier rotated, in hours (v3 Req 48.4, 48.5).
# This is not a lifetime: it is the width of the window a captured cookie stays useful for. Smaller
# means more rotations and a shorter window, at the cost of one write.
AUTH_SESSION_UPDATE_HOURS=24

# AUTH_SESSION_GRACE_SECONDS — optional, defaults to 30
# How long the identifier a rotation replaced stays valid, in seconds. It exists for one case: two
# browser tabs polling the session at the same instant both try to renew, and without a grace window
# the one that loses presents a token the server has never heard of — which signs *every* tab out.
# Set to 0 to rotate with no grace at all, accepting that.
AUTH_SESSION_GRACE_SECONDS=30

# AUTH_SIGNIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS — optional, defaults to 5
# How many failed sign-ins one email address may make inside the window below before it is refused
# (v3 Req 30.7). The limit is per address rather than per IP, because the attack it exists against
# is guessing one person's password. Set to 0 to disable.
AUTH_SIGNIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5

# AUTH_SIGNIN_WINDOW_SECONDS — optional, defaults to 900
# The window those attempts are counted over, in seconds. 900 is fifteen minutes.
AUTH_SIGNIN_WINDOW_SECONDS=900

# ── Social sign-in (TICKET-AUTH-02) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both providers below are **independently optional**. With a pair unset that provider's button is
# absent and nothing else changes; with neither set the application is exactly what AUTH-01 built.
# This is also the only account recovery there is — with no password reset (see D12), a linked
# Google or Discord identity is the way back into an account whose password is lost.
#
# The redirect URI to register with each provider is a path on whichever host you serve from:
#
#   Google   <your origin>/api/auth/callback/google
#   Discord  <your origin>/api/auth/callback/discord
#
# There is deliberately no variable here spelling that origin out (v3 Req 47.7) — AUTH_ALLOWED_HOSTS
# names the hosts this server answers on and the callback URL is built from the request.

# AUTH_ALLOWED_HOSTS — optional, but REQUIRED once either provider below is configured
# Comma-separated hostnames this deployment answers on, e.g. `dnd.example.com,localhost:3000`.
# Wildcards are allowed (`*.example.com`). This is not an origin to talk to: it is the subject line
# of this server, the way a certificate names its own hosts. It exists because an OAuth redirect URI
# has to be absolute, and a callback origin derived from an unbounded `Host` header is one an
# attacker can steer. Leave it unset if you are not using social sign-in — an empty list is what
# keeps a copied-and-not-edited .env behaving exactly as it did before this ticket.
AUTH_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID — optional
# The OAuth client id from the Google Cloud console. Set both halves or neither: half a pair is a
# start-up failure naming the missing half, rather than a silently disabled button.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET — optional
# The secret paired with GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. Server-only — it is read here and nowhere else, and the
# boundary test in src/server/ proves no client module can reach it.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# DISCORD_CLIENT_ID — optional
# The OAuth client id from the Discord developer portal, on the same terms as the Google pair.
# Discord is here because a table that plays together is usually already a Discord server, and
# signing in as the person your group knows is worth more than a second generic button.
DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=

# DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET — optional
# The secret paired with DISCORD_CLIENT_ID. Server-only, like the Google one.
DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=
